data: {"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"**A1: Keep carts in Redis or Postgres?**\n\n**The short version:** Carts have grown too big for the session cookie, which holds about 4 KB. The cart has to move to the server. The choice is which server-side store holds it.\n\n**Example:** A shopper adds 40 items, each with a size, colour and gift note. That cart serialises to something like 6–10 KB. The browser can't store it in the cookie, so the cart breaks or disappears on the next page load.\n\n**What each option leads to:**\n\n- **Redis (a1):** An in-memory key-value store. Reads and writes are very fast, and it can expire old carts automatically (for example, after 30 days). The downsides: it's another service to run and monitor, and carts can be lost on a restart unless persistence is configured. Carts also sit outside your main data, so reporting on them means extra work.\n- **Postgres (a2, the main agent's pick):** The main database. Carts survive restarts, get backed up with everything else, and can be joined with orders and inventory. The downsides: a cart lookup is a little slower than Redis (still milliseconds), carts add write load to the main database, and you need a scheduled job to delete abandoned carts.\n\n**My recommendation: Postgres (a2).** A cart is read a few times per session, so Redis's speed gain won't be noticed. Postgres adds no new infrastructure and keeps carts durable. Choose Redis only if you already run it and expect very heavy cart traffic that would strain the main database.\n\n**Caveat:** I checked the main agent's checkout for Redis, Postgres, cookie or session code and found no matches. So I can't confirm whether the project already runs a database or cache. If it has no Postgres yet, that changes the calculation and the main agent should say so.\n\nA1 is waiting on you. Pick **Redis** or **Postgres** in the Asks panel. Once you choose, I'll resolve A1 with your decision and pass it to the main agent. Ask me anything else about the trade-offs first if you like."}}

